  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nThru Friday...Persistent rain bands will continue to push onshore \nthis morning increasing (Flash) flood potential, especially for \nportions of central/north Brevard County. A Flood Watch remains in\neffect almost areawide thru Fri. Continue to keep Lake County out\nof the Flood watch given less soil saturation and better ability \nto absorb several inches over a few days. Deep moisture persists \ncombined with a disturbance aloft to produce NMRS-WSPD (60-100%) \nPoPs thru Fri as low-topped showery precip develops across the \nlocal coastal waters and at the coast pushing onshore. Primary \nimpacts with showers and ISOLD lightning storms are occasional \nlightning strikes, gusty winds locally 30-45 mph, and repeated \ntorrential downpours. Widespread rainfall amounts of 3-6\" have \nalready occurred near the coast and points further inland from \nIndian River County north through Brevard/Volusia counties. An \nadditional 4-8\" during this period (Fri night) across some of \nthese same locations with ISOLD 15\"+ within reach, esp near the \ncoast. Will also continue to message 3-6\" rainfall totals for this\nevent across our western-most coverage warning area. Do not drive\nthrough flooded roadways if you encounter flooding!\n\nA Moderate risk (level 3 out of 4) for excessive rainfall \ncontinues through Friday along portions of the ECFL Atlc coast \nwith Slight risk (Level 2 out of 4) for much of the interior. \n\nA tight easterly (onshore) pressure gradient will keep windy and \ngusty conditions in place. Sustained winds of 15-25 mph will gust \nto 35 mph with occasional gusts to 40 mph at the coast. Winds \nremain elevated at night over the interior 10-15 mph and continued\nbreezy/gusty during the night along the coast. \n\nA High Surf Advisory remains in effect through Sat as winds and \nseas build over the local coastal waters. Rough, pounding surf \nwill promote moderate to locally major beach erosion along the \ncoast. In addition, a HIGH rip current threat will exist. Entering\nthe rough and dangerous surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nOn Fri, low pressure is forecast to develop near or over the \nnorthern Bahamas. The Hurricane Center has increased probabilities\nfor subtropical/tropical development to 60% in its Tropical\nWeather Outlook. Reconnaissance aircraft are being scheduled this\nweekend. There is a lot of uncertainty how close this low will be\nto the FL east coast and how quickly it lifts away. Until the low\nforms and there is a trackable feature, specific impacts are not \nknown with much confidence. A track closer to the coast would \nincrease rainfall and wind threats; farther offshore could wrap \nsome drier air into the area this weekend. \n\nHigh temperatures generally in the L-U70s with lows continuing \nwell into the 60s to around 70F.\n\nSat-Wed...Models remain in fairly good agreement of developing low \npressure near the northern Bahamas by early Fri, lifting this \nfeature northward along the FL east coast on Sat. ERLY winds will\ngradually decrease into the weekend with direction becoming \nnortheast/north Fri afternoon-Sat morning, then further \"backing\" \nto the northwest/west Sat evening into early Sun. Expect \nlighter/variable winds next week as the pgrad relaxes once the low\nmoves away. This should pull in some drier air over the area Sat \nnight-Tue.\n\nRain chances decrease to scattered (25-50%) on Sat, with drier \nconditions yet on Sun into Mon. Scattered shower activity may \ngradually come back into the forecast Tue-Wed. Max temperatures \nwill mostly range from the M70s to L80s (south) during this time, \nwith mins generally in the L-M60s, except U50s possible for areas \nnorth/west of I-4.",
